                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tracheostomy_tube_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "1. Introduction",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tracheostomy_tube_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A tracheostomy tube is a curved tube inserted through a surgically or percutaneously created opening in the anterior neck into the trachea. The opening is called a tracheostomy stoma."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It provides an airway for ventilation, facilitates management of respiratory secretions and may be used when prolonged airway support is required."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Tracheostomy tubes are commonly encountered in intensive care, ENT, respiratory medicine, emergency care and surgical wards."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Key OSCE identification point: A curved tube with a neck flange, usually secured with a tracheostomy tie. Depending on the design, it may have an inner cannula, an obturator, an inflatable cuff and a pilot balloon."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A tracheostomy tube is different from an endotracheal tube because it enters the trachea through the neck rather than through the mouth or nose."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tracheostomy_tube_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "2. Classification",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tracheostomy_tube_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Tracheostomy tubes can be classified according to several features:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Cuff: Cuffed or uncuffed.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Cannula design: Single cannula or double cannula.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Fenestration: Fenestrated or non-fenestrated.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Material: Commonly medical-grade PVC, silicone or metal in selected designs.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Length and shape: Standard, extended-length or adjustable-flange designs.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Purpose: Routine airway maintenance, ventilation, secretion management or selected speech and weaning strategies.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The most important distinctions for OSCEs are cuffed versus uncuffed, and single versus double cannula."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tracheostomy_tube_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "3. Parts and labelling",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tracheostomy_tube_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/tracheostomy_tube_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "digitaltheatrearchive.com"
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Part",
                    "Function"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Outer cannula",
                      "Main tube that maintains the tracheostomy airway."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Inner cannula",
                      "Removable internal tube, when present, that can be cleaned or replaced according to the model and local protocol."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Neck flange or faceplate",
                      "Rests against the neck and provides attachment points for securing ties."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Obturator",
                      "Facilitates insertion by providing a smoother leading tip; removed after placement."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Proximal connector",
                      "Connects to compatible breathing circuits and ventilation equipment."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Cuff",
                      "Inflatable balloon that seals against the tracheal wall in cuffed models."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Inflation line",
                      "Connects the cuff to its inflation system."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Pilot balloon and valve",
                      "Permit cuff inflation and deflation; the pilot balloon indicates the state of the inflation system."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Fenestration",
                      "Opening in selected tubes that can permit airflow towards the upper airway when the configuration is appropriate."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Neck ties or holder",
                      "Secure the tube to prevent displacement."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Not all tracheostomy tubes contain every component. Always identify the features present on the specimen."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tracheostomy_tube_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "4. Types of tracheostomy tubes",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tracheostomy_tube_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/tracheostomy_tube_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A. Cuffed tracheostomy tube"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Has an inflatable cuff near the distal end.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Can provide a seal for positive-pressure ventilation.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Helps reduce gross leakage of secretions around the tube, but does not eliminate aspiration.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Cuff pressure must be monitored appropriately.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/tracheostomy_tube_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "B. Uncuffed tracheostomy tube"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Has no inflatable cuff.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "May be suitable for selected patients who do not require a cuff seal for ventilation.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Permits airflow around the tube when anatomy and tube size allow.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Does not provide the same seal as a cuffed tube.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/tracheostomy_tube_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "C. Double-cannula tube"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Has an outer cannula and a removable inner cannula.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The inner cannula can be cleaned or replaced, depending on the manufacturer's instructions.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Can help manage blockage from secretions.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The inner cannula reduces the effective internal diameter and must be compatible with the intended ventilation system.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/tracheostomy_tube_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "D. Single-cannula tube"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Has one principal cannula.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "May have a relatively large internal diameter for a given outer diameter.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Does not have a removable inner cannula to clear separately.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Blockage management depends on the specific device and clinical protocol.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/tracheostomy_tube_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "E. Fenestrated tracheostomy tube"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Contains one or more openings in the curved portion of the tube.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "May facilitate airflow through the upper airway and support speech or weaning in selected patients.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Requires the correct inner-cannula configuration and cuff status.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Must not be used indiscriminately during ventilation because the openings can cause leakage or alter airflow.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Other designs include adjustable-flange tubes, extended-length tubes for particular neck anatomies, and specialised tubes designed for speech or other clinical needs."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tracheostomy_tube_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "5. Principle of operation",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tracheostomy_tube_group_6",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A tracheostomy tube maintains a patent route between the external environment and the trachea."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "The tube is positioned through the tracheostomy stoma.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The flange rests against the neck.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Ties or a holder secure the tube.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The lumen allows airflow and may connect to a ventilator.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A cuff, when present and inflated appropriately, creates a seal against the tracheal wall.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "An inner cannula, when present, can be removed for cleaning or replacement according to the device instructions.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A tracheostomy tube bypasses the upper airway, so humidification is particularly important because the nose and upper airway no longer warm and humidify inspired gas in the usual way."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tracheostomy_tube_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "6. Indications and clinical uses",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tracheostomy_tube_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Indication",
                    "Rationale"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Prolonged mechanical ventilation",
                      "Provides an alternative airway when extended ventilatory support is needed."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Upper-airway obstruction",
                      "Bypasses an obstruction above the tracheostomy, when appropriate."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Airway protection in selected patients",
                      "Supports airway management when clinically indicated."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Secretion management",
                      "Provides access for tracheal suctioning."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Weaning from ventilation",
                      "May facilitate gradual reduction of ventilatory support in suitable patients."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Selected head and neck operations",
                      "Provides airway access or helps manage postoperative airway requirements."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Long-term airway support",
                      "May be used in selected neurological, respiratory or structural conditions."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Tracheostomy is not automatically required for every patient needing prolonged ventilation. The decision depends on the clinical condition, prognosis, anticipated duration of airway support and risks of the procedure."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tracheostomy_tube_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "7. Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tracheostomy_tube_group_8",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Provides a stable route for respiratory support in selected patients.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Can reduce the need for a tube passing through the mouth or nose.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Facilitates tracheal secretion suctioning.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "May improve comfort during prolonged airway management compared with an oral ETT in suitable patients.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Can facilitate communication and oral intake in selected patients when airway function and swallowing have been assessed.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "May support ventilator weaning and rehabilitation.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tracheostomy_tube_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "8. Disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tracheostomy_tube_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Requires a surgical or percutaneous procedure to establish the stoma.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Can cause bleeding, infection and tracheal injury.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Requires regular care, humidification and secretion management.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The tube may become blocked or displaced.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A cuff may cause pressure injury if excessively inflated.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Speech and swallowing may be impaired, depending on the patient's condition and tube configuration.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Accidental displacement can be particularly dangerous in a newly formed stoma.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tracheostomy_tube_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "9. Complications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tracheostomy_tube_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Complication",
                    "Key points"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Tube obstruction",
                      "Thick secretions, crusting, blood or a blocked inner cannula may impair airflow."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Accidental decannulation",
                      "The tube becomes displaced from the trachea."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Bleeding",
                      "May occur during insertion or from later trauma."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Stomal infection",
                      "Redness, discharge, pain or systemic features may occur."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Subcutaneous emphysema",
                      "Air enters the tissues around the stoma or neck."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Pneumothorax",
                      "Rare but serious; may occur after insertion or due to associated injury."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Tracheal mucosal injury",
                      "May result from excessive cuff pressure or tube movement."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Tracheal stenosis",
                      "A possible late consequence of airway injury."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Tracheoesophageal fistula",
                      "Rare complication associated with pressure injury or other tissue damage."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Aspiration",
                      "Can occur despite a cuffed tube."
                    ],
                    [
                      "Tracheal erosion or major vascular injury",
                      "Rare but potentially catastrophic complications."
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Emergency warning: Acute respiratory distress in a patient with a tracheostomy should be treated as an airway emergency. Call for experienced airway assistance immediately and follow the local emergency tracheostomy algorithm. Do not assume that oxygen delivered to the face will adequately oxygenate a patient whose upper airway is bypassed or obstructed."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tracheostomy_tube_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "10. Tracheostomy tube care: high-yield OSCE station",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tracheostomy_tube_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Routine care is essential to prevent obstruction, infection and accidental displacement."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/tracheostomy_tube_09.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 1 — Assessment"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assess respiratory rate, oxygen saturation, work of breathing, tube position, secretion burden, stoma condition and the patient's overall stability. Check the documented tube type and size."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/tracheostomy_tube_10.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 2 — Humidification"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Provide appropriate humidification according to the patient's needs. Monitor secretion thickness and ensure the prescribed oxygen delivery system is functioning."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/tracheostomy_tube_11.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 3 — Suctioning when indicated"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Assess the need for suctioning rather than performing it automatically. Use appropriate technique, monitoring and suction settings according to local protocol. Avoid unnecessarily prolonged or repeated suction passes."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/tracheostomy_tube_12.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 4 — Inner cannula care"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "If the device has a removable inner cannula, clean or replace it according to the manufacturer's instructions and local protocol. Maintain airway patency throughout the process; some inner cannulas are disposable and must be replaced rather than cleaned."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/tracheostomy_tube_13.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Step 5 — Stoma and fixation care"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Inspect the stoma and surrounding skin, provide appropriate cleaning and dressing, and check that the tube holder is secure without excessive pressure. Tube changes require appropriate training and precautions, especially in a newly formed stoma."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tracheostomy_tube_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "11. Emergency management: blocked or displaced tracheostomy tube",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tracheostomy_tube_group_12",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "This is one of the most important tracheostomy OSCE scenarios."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "tracheostomy_tube_group_13",
              "group_title": "Recognising obstruction or displacement",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Look for:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Respiratory distress, falling oxygen saturation or cyanosis.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Reduced or absent airflow through the tube.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Difficulty passing a suction catheter.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Absent or markedly reduced exhaled carbon dioxide in a ventilated patient.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "A displaced tube, bleeding, swelling or surgical emphysema.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "tracheostomy_tube_group_14",
              "group_title": "Immediate priorities",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Call for help and activate the emergency response.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Give oxygen and assess the patient's ability to breathe.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Remove any speaking valve or cap, if present.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Remove a removable inner cannula if obstruction is suspected and the device design permits this.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Attempt to pass a suction catheter according to the emergency algorithm. Failure to pass it suggests obstruction or displacement.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "If the tube is blocked or displaced, follow the local emergency tracheostomy algorithm. In a patient with a mature, well-established stoma, trained clinicians may need to remove a non-functioning tube and restore ventilation by an appropriate route.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "If ventilation is inadequate, provide rescue ventilation and oxygenation using the appropriate upper-airway or stoma route, depending on the anatomy, patency and clinical circumstances.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "7."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Critical distinction: A newly formed tracheostomy stoma can close rapidly or create a false passage during attempted reinsertion. Blind reinsertion into a fresh stoma can be dangerous. Management must follow the emergency algorithm and involve experienced airway personnel."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tracheostomy_tube_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "12. Cuffed versus uncuffed tracheostomy tubes",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tracheostomy_tube_group_15",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Feature",
                    "Cuffed",
                    "Uncuffed"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Inflatable cuff",
                      "Present",
                      "Absent"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Ventilator seal",
                      "Can provide a seal when appropriately inflated",
                      "No cuff seal"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Air leakage",
                      "Reduced when cuff is inflated appropriately",
                      "Depends on tube fit and airflow"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Aspiration prevention",
                      "Does not eliminate aspiration",
                      "Does not provide cuff-related protection"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Typical use",
                      "Selected ventilated patients and other specific indications",
                      "Selected patients who do not need a cuff seal"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Important concern",
                      "Excessive cuff pressure causes injury",
                      "Airflow and ventilation must be assessed for suitability"
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tracheostomy_tube_subsection_14",
          "subsection_title": "13. High-yield OSCE viva questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tracheostomy_tube_group_16",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. Identify this instrument."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A tracheostomy tube, used to maintain an airway through a tracheostomy stoma."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What is the function of the flange?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It rests against the neck and provides attachment points for the tube holder."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. Why is an inner cannula useful?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It can be removed for cleaning or replacement, helping manage secretions and maintain tube patency."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. What is the function of the cuff?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It provides a seal against the tracheal wall to facilitate positive-pressure ventilation and reduce gross leakage around the tube."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. What is the difference between a tracheostomy and a tracheotomy?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Tracheotomy refers to the incision into the trachea, whereas tracheostomy commonly refers to the creation of an opening into the trachea and is also used to describe the resulting stoma."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. Why is humidification important?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The tracheostomy bypasses the normal upper-airway warming and humidification process, so secretions can become thick and obstruct the tube without appropriate humidification."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. What are the immediate concerns in a blocked tracheostomy?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Airway obstruction, hypoxaemia and respiratory arrest. Call for help, administer oxygen, assess tube patency and follow the emergency tracheostomy algorithm."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. Why is a displaced tube in a fresh stoma particularly dangerous?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The tract may not be established, so blind reinsertion can create a false passage and prevent effective ventilation."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q9. Does an inflated cuff prevent aspiration completely?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. Aspiration can still occur, including from secretions that collect above the cuff."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
